; 08-proof.kurt

; `proof` opens an indented block right after a `show`.  Inside, you may
; write as many intermediate steps as you like -- each one is checked against
; the theory so far, exactly like at the top level.  `qed` (09-qed.kurt)
; closes the block and re-derives the goal using everything established
; inside it.

bool A, B, C

use A implies B
use B implies C
use A

show C
proof
    B          ; an intermediate step, not the goal itself
    C          ; now the goal follows
qed

; A `proof` block doesn't have to restate the goal as its last line -- `qed`
; simply tries to derive the goal fresh, using the full theory at that point.
; So a block that only adds redundant information still closes just fine, as
; long as the goal has become derivable somehow:

show B
proof
    use A      ; redundant -- `A` was already known -- yet still allowed
qed
